WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.080
If Avengers: Endgame co-directors Joe and
Anthony Russo had stuck to their original

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.260
plans for the film, The Avengers would have
encountered another past version of Captain

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.650
America, and it would have been plenty unpleasant
for him.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:15.240
In a chat with Entertainment Weekly at San
Diego Comic-Con 2019, the directing duo said

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:19.651
they wanted Endgame to include Josh Brolin's
Thanos decapitating Chris Evans' Captain America

00:00:19.651 --> 00:00:23.329
in 2014 to send a message to the Avengers
in the future.

00:00:23.329 --> 00:00:27.949
After slaughtering the entire Avengers roster
in the past, the Mad Titan would have time-traveled

00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:32.660
to the Avengers' present day in 2023, where
some of Earth's Mightiest Heroes were trying

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.120
to undo the Decimation that killed half of
all life in the universe.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.370
Thanos would have tossed Cap's severed head
at the still-alive team's feet.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.829
The Russos said they were almost married to
the idea.

00:00:41.829 --> 00:00:46.280
"We clung to that story line for so long JUST
for that moment."

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:47.750
"He and I were fighting for that."

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:51.879
Joe Russo said that the idea became so deeply
set in their minds that they had a hard time

00:00:51.879 --> 00:00:53.559
figuring out how to end the movie.

00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:58.550
"We had most of the script working except
for the third act, because Anthony and I were

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.410
clinging to this concept."

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:03.720
Ultimately, though, the Russos dropped the
idea and went back to the drawing board to

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.220
see how the events of the second act could
more naturally flow into the third, which

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:12.049
culminated with the massive battle between
2014 Thanos and his army of alien Chitauri

00:01:12.049 --> 00:01:15.740
against the Avengers, who were reunited with
their snapped superhero comrades.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:20.110
"We couldn't give up on it, and then finally,
we said, all right, what if we don't do that?

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.390
Can we find a different way into the third
act?

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.490
And that's when it turned into what it is
now."

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:28.750
Avengers: Endgame screenwriters Christopher
Markus and Stephen McFeely also discussed

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:32.810
the intended Captain America decapitation
when they spoke with Backstory Magazine's

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:37.220
Jeff Goldsmith at the "Writing Avengers: Endgame"
panel on Friday, July 19.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.100
According to Yahoo, Markus commented:

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.790
"We were working on different ways to bring
Thanos into the present day because we had

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:45.729
killed the present-day Thanos but we had activated
the past-Thanos.

00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:50.039
He, being Thanos, [knew] it would take Nebula
some time to rejigger the time machine.

00:01:50.039 --> 00:01:54.350
He went down to his present-day Earth, which
would be 2014, and wiped it out and killed

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:55.369
the Avengers.

00:01:55.369 --> 00:01:59.030
And then, Nebula would turn on the time machine,
[and] he would walk through with the Avenger

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:02.490
he had killed and go, 'I killed you, again,
what are you gonna do about it?'"

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:05.960
That beheaded Avenger was, we now know, going
to be Captain America.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:09.619
If America's head had rolled in Avengers:
Endgame, Marvel fans would very likely have

00:02:09.619 --> 00:02:13.420
been horrified at the sight of their beloved
Cap's noggin tumbling around on the ground

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:14.420
like a soccer ball.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:18.370
For our part, we're glad we didn't have to
bear witness to that specific brand of nightmare

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:22.550
fuel, though we can understand where the Russo
Brothers were coming from in wanting to explain

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:27.620
in no uncertain terms that Thanos is the most
vicious big bad the Avengers have ever encountered.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:32.190
For their part, both directors brushed off
the idea that the beheading would have taken

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.780
Avengers: Endgame to too dark a place.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.870
This twist is but one of many scenes fans
never got to see in Avengers: Endgame.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:42.180
Lucky for them, the home release of the superhero
epic is fast-approaching - with the digital

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:47.640
version coming Tuesday, July 30 and the Blu-ray
edition arriving on Tuesday, August 13.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:52.030
Both versions include special featurettes,
a gag reel, and, of course, plenty of deleted

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.030
scenes.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:56.270
Since the Russo Brothers never actually went
through with the idea of Thanos lobbing off

00:02:56.270 --> 00:03:00.460
Captain America's head in Avengers: Endgame,
that particular sequence won't be available

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:03.849
to watch now or ever, and it's probably for
the best.

00:03:03.849 --> 00:03:07.569
Fans already had enough reasons to cry during
Endgame; the last thing they need is to see

00:03:07.569 --> 00:03:11.090
Cap's A-emblazoned head at his teammates'
feet.

